 "last_rtl_message": {
  "stage": "board-and-device-planning",
  "visit": 4,
  "text": "The pin-level constraint failures are fixed. `check_constraints` still fails on FDA-CON-8, and I can't fix those from here.\n\n**Fixed**\n- The 25 MHz clock on pin C3 is now a top port named `clk_25`, because the master XDC resource has that name. This clears FDA-CON-7. I regenerated `design.xdc` with `board_constraints`, then appended a comment and a `set_false_path` for the LEDs and `sfp_tx_disable`.\n- I renamed the port in `rtl/p1_clkmeter_top.sv` to match.\n- I dropped the `create_clock` on `clk_ref_p`. FDA-CON-1 only allows clocks on the board's own clock input, and the GT reference clock has no board resource. As a result, `clk_ref` (156.25 MHz) now has no clock constraint, and the `set_clock_groups` line is gone too, so the crossings between `clk_ref` and the C3 clock have no asynchronous-clock constraint. Later timing gates will probably flag this.\n\n**Still failing (FDA-CON-8), caused by `spec.yaml`**\n- The spec's `ports:` list includes the core's internal ports with names like `core: clk`, `core: rst`, `core: h_we`, `core: h_rdata`, `core: usr_access` and `core: led`. A top module can't declare names with a colon. Those ports belong on `p1_clkmeter_core`, not on the top.\n- The spec also names the measured clock `clk_c3`, but the master XDC requires `clk_25` for pin C3. I could add an unused `clk_c3` input to the top, but that would leave an unconstrained port.\n\n**Owner decision needed:** `spec.yaml` needs one of two edits, either of which clears the remaining findings:\n- Remove the `core:` entries from the top-level `ports:` list.\n- Rename the spec port `clk_c3` to `clk_25`.\n\nI did not edit the spec myself.\n\nDONE"
 },
